Author: Martin Sedlacek <martin.martin.sedlacek@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 14 01:38:40 2026 +0200

    Match on identity, not name, when pruning the object-init queue
    
    Simulator._pre_remove_object drops the object being removed from the global
    og.sim._objects_to_initialize by comparing NAMES. Object names are unique per
    SCENE -- Scene.add_object asserts against that scene's registry and nothing
    wider -- so as soon as several scenes share one stage the comparison is not
    specific enough to identify the entry.
    
    In a vector env every member is typically built from the same config, so all N
    scenes hold a "corkscrew", a "cube", and so on. Remove one of them while a
    sibling's same-named object is still queued and the loop pops whichever was
    queued FIRST, which is the sibling's. Two things then go wrong at once: the
    sibling's object stays on the stage and in its own registry but is never
    initialized, and the object actually being removed keeps its slot and gets
    initialize()d after its prim has already been deleted. The last member is always
    fine, since nothing is removed after it -- the failure presents as "every scene
    but the last one".
    
    Neither half surfaces where it happens. The uninitialized object raises "Object
    must be initialized before dumping state!" from an unrelated scene.dump_state()
    much later, or "Cannot update uninitialized state." from inside play() itself,
    which initializes the queue and then updates every object's states. The stale
    entry raises "prim view ['/World/scene_1/receiver/base_link'] is not a valid
    view" out of _non_physics_step.
    
    Identity is strictly narrower than the name test and is always the correct
    entry: the object handed to _pre_remove_object is the one that was queued.
    (scene, name) would be equivalent, since add_object already forbids two live
    same-named objects in one scene.
    
    Predates this fork -- the loop is unchanged from OG 1.1.1 and is present in
    stock 3.9.1.
    
    Verified against REALM's vector env at num_envs=2, three resets plus a warmup
    reset, with REALM's own workaround of the time (RealmVectorEnvironment._repair_init_queue,
    since REMOVED -- it exists in no .py in the tree) left in place so it could
    report what it found:
    
      perturbation   before                                              after
      V-SC           4 x "Re-queueing 5 object(s) evicted ... scene0/     0
                     corkscrew, table_knife, wineglass, water_glass,
                     bottle_of_wine"
      VSB-NOBJ       4 x "Re-queueing 1 object(s) evicted ... scene0/     0
                     cube"
    
    Both configurations PASS the harness; per-member object poses and contact-row
    counts are identical before and after, so the only change is that nothing is
    evicted any more.

